# Rules for the writer agent

You are writing one paper from a fixed set of sources. The paper source is `Paper/paper.md`. The `.docx` in `Output/` is generated and never edited by hand.

The human may have put an original manuscript (DOCX or PDF) in `Manuscript/` or the project root. `{{program}} import` copied its text into `Paper/paper.md`; the original is never edited, moved or locked. If `check` reports `manuscript_hash_mismatch`, stop and ask the human to re-import it.

## If the human asks how to start

Run `{{program}} help --json` and relay `data.steps`, or answer directly with the commands in the Workflow section below. This project is the folder that contains `paper.toml`. Do not search parent folders, `Documents`, `Monograph`, `AppData` or the application source to answer a usage question. Only inspect the project when the human asks about their actual files or reports an error.

## The rule that matters

Every factual sentence must be supported by text that exists in the generated cache text, and must cite a source key and a page. If the sources do not support it, do not write it. This is a hard limit, not a style preference.

The cache is outside the project. `{{program}} doctor --json` reports its paths under `data.cache`: `index`, `text` and `tmp`. Never copy the cache into the project.

You may summarise, compare and connect sources. You may not add outside knowledge, invent numbers, dates, authors or quotes, or cite a page you have not read.

## What you may touch

| Path | Read | Write |
| --- | --- | --- |
| cache text (see `doctor --json` `data.cache.text`) | yes | never |
| `References/**` (PDFs, DOCX, Zotero JSON) | yes | never |
| `References/**/<name>.context.md` (context cards) | yes | only below the marker line, see Context cards |
| `Manuscript/` (the original) | yes | never |
| `Paper/paper.md` | yes | yes |
| `Candidates/` | yes | yes |
| `Output/` | yes | never |
| `paper.toml` | yes | never |
| `.paperchain/` (the lock log) | yes | never |
| `AGENTS.md` | yes | never |

`References/` is hashed and read-only, except the context cards. If a check reports a hash mismatch, stop and tell the human. Do not try to repair it, and never run `{{program}} unlock` or `{{program}} import`.

## Workflow

1. Run `{{program}} start --json`. It sorts the files, imports the manuscript, converts and locks the references, writes the context cards and runs the diagnostics; running it again is safe. If `data.needs_human` is not empty, stop and ask the human (it names the question); when the human names the manuscript, rerun with `--manuscript "<file>"`. Never accept changed references yourself.
2. If `Paper/paper.md` is still the template, ask the human to run `{{program}} import` on the original manuscript.
3. Read the context card of each source you need (`data.cards[].card` from `start`, or `{{program}} context --json`). Read the full text in `data.cache.text` only for the pages a card points you to. Write the scoped resume for every key in `data.pending_cards`.
4. Draft in `Paper/paper.md` using the citation syntax below.
5. Run `{{program}} check --json`. Fix every error. Warnings are yours to judge.
6. Run `{{program}} build`. The document appears in `Output/`.
7. Report what you changed and which citations you added. Also report `data.reference_fingerprint` from `check --json` or `build`, and say so plainly if `check` or `build` warns `reference_set_reblessed` or `reference_fingerprint_changed`: the human decides whether that change was intended.

If the same error survives three attempts, stop and ask the human.

## Context cards

Every converted reference has a card beside it, `<name>.context.md`. The part above the marker line is generated (title, sections found, a one-line preview per page) and is rewritten when the reference changes. Never edit it.

`{{program}} context --json` lists `data.pending`: sources whose card has no scoped resume yet, or whose resume is marked `> STALE`. For each one, read its cached text once, then replace the `PENDING` line under `## Scoped resume` with a short resume written for this paper's topic: what the source is and why it matters here, its key findings with the page of each, and candidate quotes copied exactly with their page, plus any limits or caveats. If the card is stale, rewrite the resume and delete the stale line. Write only below the marker line.

A card is a set of leads, never evidence. Before you cite from it, open the cited page in the cached text and copy the quote exactly; `check` is the judge, not the card. Do not cite anything that appears only in a card.

## Citation syntax

Put the source key and page in square brackets after the sentence or claim:

- `[@smith2024]` — source only
- `[@smith2024, p. 12]` — source and page
- `[@smith2024, pp. 12-14]` — page range
- `[@smith2024, p. 12: "exact words from page 12"]` — page plus quoted evidence

The quoted form is the strongest. `check` proves that the quote appears on that page. Use it for anything that turns on specific wording, a number, a definition or a comparison. Copy the quote exactly from `data.cache.text/<key>.txt`; do not add ellipses or fix spelling.

One citation per bracket. Write `[@a, p. 1] [@b, p. 2]`, never `[@a; @b]`.

These are the only accepted forms. `[see @key]`, `[-@key]` and a bare `@key` are errors, not citations: `check` refuses to guess, because a citation it does not parse is a citation it cannot verify.

The quote is evidence, not text. It is removed from the built document. Without a CSL style the citation remains as `(Author, Year, p. 12)`; with a style such as Vancouver, Pandoc citeproc formats it and the page locator may not be printed. Keep the page in the draft either way: `check` uses it as evidence.

If the project has `References/library.json` (a CSL JSON export from Zotero), `build` uses Pandoc citeproc and that library is the authority for titles, authors and journal details. Each source records its Better BibTeX id in `citation_key`; `paperchain match` fills it in by title and reports anything it cannot match. Either the source key or the `citation_key` may be cited. If `[build] style = "vancouver"` is set, `styles/vancouver.csl` is the style file; the agent must not edit the export, the style file or `paper.toml`.

Do not add YAML front matter such as `suppress-bibliography: true` or `bibliography:`. `check` rejects it with `front_matter_overrides_citations`, because it would silently remove the reference list.

## When a source is missing

Do not fill the gap from memory. Write `Candidates/<key>.md`:

```
key: author2025
title: ...
where: https://doi.org/... or a file name
why: the claim in section 3 needs this
```

Then stop and ask the human to add it. The human downloads the file into `References/`, then runs `{{program}} convert` and `{{program}} lock`.

A candidate is not a source. You cannot cite it.

## What gets the paper rejected

- A citation key that is neither a source key nor that source's `citation_key`.
- YAML front matter that changes the bibliography or the citation output.
- A page number past the end of the source.
- A quote that does not appear on the cited page.
- Placeholders such as `TODO`, `TKTK` or `[[`.
- Facts or figures that appear nowhere in the sources.

## Tools

Allowed: `{{program}} start`, `{{program}} doctor`, `{{program}} check`, `{{program}} build`, `{{program}} context`, `git status`, `git diff`.

Not allowed: `{{program}} import`, `{{program}} convert`, `{{program}} lock`, `{{program}} unlock`, `{{program}} init`, any `--force` flag, editing any `.docx`, deleting files, installing packages, or changing `paper.toml`.

The `scout` subagent may search the web to propose candidates. It cannot write the paper.
